--- field rule.name
type: str

The policy rule that produced this record, by name. Rules are the unit an
administrator writes and edits, so this is the field that connects a record
back to something a person can change.

--- field rule.report_level
type: uint

The reporting level the matching rule asked for. Records exist only because
a rule contained an explicit report directive, and this is the level it
named.

--- field rule.layer
type: str.enum
values: raw-packet | packet | flow

Which evaluation layer the rule sat in. The layers see progressively more
context — a raw frame, a parsed packet, a tracked connection — so the layer
bounds what the rule could possibly have matched on.

--- field rule.seat
type: str.enum
values: ingress | egress | local-in | local-out

Where in the path evaluation happened. Distinguishes traffic entering or
leaving the machine from traffic terminating at or originating from a local
socket, which is the difference between forwarding policy and host policy.

--- field network.direction
type: str.enum
values: in | out

Which way the traffic was moving relative to this machine.

--- field network.interface.name
type: str

The network interface the traffic was seen on.

--- field network.interface.index
type: uint

The kernel's index for that interface. Zero when no interface was
determined. Indexes are reused across interface teardown, so
`network.interface.name` is the durable one.

--- field network.ether_type
type: uint

The link-layer protocol number of the frame.

--- field network.family
type: uint.enum

The address family of the addresses in this record. Zero when the traffic
was evaluated below the layer where addresses exist, in which case no
address, port or protocol field is present.

--- field network.protocol
type: uint.enum

The transport protocol number.

--- field network.length
type: uint.bytes

The length of the traffic unit evaluated.

--- field source.address
type: str.ip

The address the traffic came from, rendered in the textual form for its
family.

--- field source.port
type: uint

The transport port the traffic came from. Present only for protocols that
have ports.

--- field destination.address
type: str.ip

The address the traffic was going to, rendered in the textual form for its
family.

--- field destination.port
type: uint

The transport port the traffic was going to. Present only for protocols
that have ports.

--- field flow.state
type: str.enum
values: new | established | related | invalid | untracked

How connection tracking classified this traffic. `untracked` means tracking
was not applied rather than that tracking failed; `invalid` means it was
applied and the traffic did not fit any known flow.

--- field policy.generation
type: uint

The generation counter of the rule set in force when the decision was made.
Increments on every policy load, so a record can be tied to the exact rule
set that produced it rather than to whatever is loaded when the record is
read.

--- event ntfe.report
origin: pnp
tier: standard
gating: an explicit report directive in the matching rule
cardinality: once per matching evaluation
emitters: pnp/report.c

The record of a traffic policy decision that a rule explicitly asked to be
reported. Not a record of every decision — the engine evaluates constantly
and reports only where a rule says to.

**A stock machine emits none of these.** The shipped baseline rule set
contains no report directives, so this event exists and never fires until
an administrator adds one. Absence of these records says nothing about
whether traffic was evaluated.

The engine's own record of every verdict goes to a private ring read by the
userspace policy daemon, not to the event stream, and is overwritten when
no reader is attached. So the complete decision history is neither in the
event stream nor durable.

Address, port and protocol fields are conditional on how far up the stack
evaluation reached: traffic matched at the raw-packet layer has no
addresses to record.

field: rule.name                     required
field: rule.report_level             required
field: rule.layer                    required
field: rule.seat                     required
field: outcome.verdict               required
field: outcome.reason                when outcome.verdict == reject
  Whether the refusal was prohibited or refused — the two flavours of an
  explicit rejection.
field: network.direction             required
field: network.interface.name        required
field: network.interface.index       required
field: network.ether_type            required
field: network.family                required
field: network.protocol              when network.family != 0
field: source.address                when network.family != 0
field: destination.address           when network.family != 0
field: source.port                   when the protocol has ports
field: destination.port              when the protocol has ports
field: flow.state                    when flow tracking applied
field: network.length                required
field: policy.generation             required
